<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gamla Uppsala</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Old Uppsala</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gamla <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> is a village and ancient royal site north of modern Uppsala in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Its great burial mounds date from the 5th and 6th centuries, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adam-of-bremen">Adam of Bremen</a> described a pagan temple there in the 11th century. It was the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/svear">Svear</a> kings and from 1164 of Sweden's first archbishopric, moved to the present Uppsala in the 13th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gamla-uppsala.jpg" alt="Gamla Uppsala" width="320" height="141" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: OlofE · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gamla_uppsala.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Gamla <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> lies on the Fyris Wolds, a cultivated plain in the valley of the River Fyris. People have been buried there for about 2,000 years, since the land rose above the water. Cairns reveal settlement in the Nordic Bronze Age, but most grave fields date from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>. There were once between 2,000 and 3,000 mounds, most now turned into farmland, gardens and quarries; about 250 barrows remain. The Royal Mounds are three large barrows, dated to the 5th and 6th centuries and now called the Eastern, Middle and Western Mounds.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>From the 3rd and 4th centuries Gamla Uppsala was an important religious, economic and political centre. Early Scandinavian sources, such as Ynglingatal, speak of the king of the Swedes as the &quot;King at Uppsala&quot;. It was the site of the Thing of all Swedes, held at the end of February or early March. Adam of Bremen described the Temple at Uppsala in the 1070s as a pagan centre with wooden statues of Odin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thor">Thor</a> and Freyr; his account is contested. In the 1080s the Christian king Ingi was exiled for refusing to perform the sacrifices, and Blot-Sweyn was elected, until Ingi killed him and regained the throne.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s first archbishopric was placed at Gamla Uppsala in 1164, but the village had lost its access to navigable water as the land rose, and the archiepiscopal seat moved to Östra Aros, later renamed Uppsala, in 1273. In 1846 Bror Emil Hildebrand excavated the Eastern Mound and found burnt bones and bronze panels that probably adorned a helmet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vendel">Vendel</a> type; in 1874 he opened the Western Mound, which held a Frankish sword with gold and garnets.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Gamla <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a> is a parish and village outside the city of Uppsala. Gamla Uppsala Church, begun probably in the 11th century and finished in the 12th, stands on a site where archaeologists have found the remains of large wooden buildings; some hold that these were the pagan temple, others an early wooden church. The astronomer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anders-celsius">Anders Celsius</a> was buried there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">Pope John Paul II</a> held an open-air mass at the Royal Mounds in 1989, and the Swedish Asatru Society resumed holding blóts there in 2000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gamla Uppsala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The western mound at <b>Gamla Uppsala</b>, where Snorri Sturluson placed the burial of Eadgils, held a powerful man buried around 575.</blockquote><a href="/work/beowulf">Beowulf · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Tacitus wrote of the Suiones in the 1st century, and by the 6th century the Svear had a power centre at <b>Old Uppsala</b>, where great royal mounds were raised.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-the-swedes">Kingdom of the Swedes</a></li></ul></section>
